 2. Gates: Some jobs could be off-limits to AI 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Bill Gates says governments may eventually need to do something that runs counter to centuries of technological progress: deliberately stop machines from replacing people in some jobs.

 Why it matters: Gates expects AI to eventually outperform humans at such a wide range of work that simply retraining displaced employees won't be enough.

 In his new essay on the impact of AI, Gates proposes the notion of "Human Reserved" jobs: occupations or tasks that society chooses to leave to people even when AI or robots could perform them. Gates says the idea is loosely analogous to nature reserves where societies could develop certain lands but choose not to because doing so would destroy something worth preserving. Zoom in: In an interview with Axios, Gates floated child care and jury service as among the types of things humans are clearly better suited for, but said there should be room for humans even in areas where AI also has a role to play.

 In education and health care, Gates said, "you wouldn't want to completely ban" AI. Instead, a profession might be protected while workers use AI to improve their output. Between the lines: In the essay , Gates also recommends taxing AI tokens and robots to lessen the economic incentives that companies currently have to automate human work.

 "The tax system nudges you toward replacing people with machines," he writes. Keep reading. 

 
 
